1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,270
On September seventh, 1945,

2
00:00:03,290 --> 00:00:06,603
a Catholic bishop sat down to lunch in a

3
00:00:06,623 --> 00:00:09,566
prisoner of war camp in the Texas Panhandle.

4
00:00:09,586 --> 00:00:12,528
He had asked to eat exactly what the

5
00:00:12,548 --> 00:00:13,269
prisoners ate.

6
00:00:13,289 --> 00:00:15,861
They served him a bowl of thin,

7
00:00:15,881 --> 00:00:18,454
watery soup with pieces of gummy spaghetti

8
00:00:18,474 --> 00:00:21,046
in it, made with almost no salt.

9
00:00:21,066 --> 00:00:22,898
Then a dry, over-salted herring.

10
00:00:22,918 --> 00:00:24,379
Then bread and water.

11
00:00:24,399 --> 00:00:25,510
That was all.

12
00:00:26,725 --> 00:00:28,945
He watched the prisoners around him save

13
00:00:28,965 --> 00:00:31,505
their soup, and dip the salty fish into

14
00:00:31,525 --> 00:00:33,745
it, to give the soup some taste.

15
00:00:33,765 --> 00:00:36,305
Later he walked into the kitchen to see

16
00:00:36,325 --> 00:00:37,905
what was coming for dinner.

17
00:00:37,925 --> 00:00:39,825
He found tubs of pig's feet,

18
00:00:39,845 --> 00:00:41,105
sacks of moldy-looking potatoes,

19
00:00:41,125 --> 00:00:43,045
and a basket of overripe tomatoes.

20
00:00:44,575 --> 00:00:47,180
The men at those tables were Italian soldiers.

21
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,477
For months they had been killing and

22
00:00:49,497 --> 00:00:51,774
eating the dogs and cats in their

23
00:00:51,794 --> 00:00:53,086
compounds, and whatever birds,

24
00:00:53,106 --> 00:00:55,055
lizards and rattlesnakes they could catch.

25
00:00:55,075 --> 00:00:57,680
This is the documented story of the hunger

26
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:00,305
at Camp Hereford, and of the small Texas

27
00:01:00,325 --> 00:01:02,274
church that fed some of them,

28
00:01:02,294 --> 00:01:04,571
in exchange for paintings that are still

29
00:01:04,591 --> 00:01:05,575
on its walls.

30
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:10,592
Hereford was a farm town of about two

31
00:01:10,612 --> 00:01:12,223
thousand three hundred people,

32
00:01:12,243 --> 00:01:14,262
forty-five miles southwest of Amarillo.

33
00:01:14,282 --> 00:01:17,431
In 1942 the Army built one of the largest

34
00:01:17,451 --> 00:01:19,810
prisoner of war camps in the country just

35
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:22,189
outside it, on flat open land where an

36
00:01:22,209 --> 00:01:24,290
escaping man could be seen for miles.

37
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:30,190
The first prisoners arrived in April 1943.

38
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:32,403
They had been captured in North Africa,

39
00:01:32,423 --> 00:01:34,616
shipped through the Panama Canal to the

40
00:01:34,636 --> 00:01:36,830
West Coast, carried by train to Texas,

41
00:01:36,850 --> 00:01:39,043
and marched on foot to the gate.

42
00:01:39,063 --> 00:01:40,940
Almost all of them were Italian.

43
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:43,153
At times there were more than three

44
00:01:43,173 --> 00:01:45,070
thousand of them behind the wire.

45
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:49,910
In September 1943, Italy surrendered and

46
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:50,770
changed sides.

47
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:53,780
Its new government asked the prisoners in

48
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:56,360
America to work with the Allies.

49
00:01:56,380 --> 00:01:58,940
More than eighty-five percent of the

50
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:01,090
fifty-one thousand Italian prisoners in

51
00:02:01,110 --> 00:02:02,380
the country agreed.

52
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:05,390
Those who refused called themselves the Nons.

53
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:08,420
Many of them were sent to Hereford.

54
00:02:10,125 --> 00:02:12,404
At first, the Nons ate well.

55
00:02:12,424 --> 00:02:14,702
Under Article eleven of the Geneva

56
00:02:14,722 --> 00:02:17,384
Convention, prisoners had to be fed the

57
00:02:17,404 --> 00:02:19,683
same food, in the same quantity,

58
00:02:19,703 --> 00:02:20,832
as American troops.

59
00:02:20,852 --> 00:02:24,358
A local newspaper in 1943 listed what was

60
00:02:24,378 --> 00:02:25,590
on their plates:

61
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:27,234
apple butter, tomato sauce,

62
00:02:27,254 --> 00:02:29,425
chicken cacciatore.

63
00:02:29,445 --> 00:02:31,105
Many of the men gained weight.

64
00:02:32,375 --> 00:02:34,943
They earned camp scrip working on the

65
00:02:34,963 --> 00:02:36,791
farms around Hereford, planting potatoes

66
00:02:36,811 --> 00:02:39,379
and harvesting carrots, and spent it in

67
00:02:39,399 --> 00:02:40,858
the canteen on cigarettes,

68
00:02:40,878 --> 00:02:42,336
candy bars and beer.

69
00:02:42,356 --> 00:02:44,415
They played soccer and bocce,

70
00:02:44,435 --> 00:02:46,735
staged musicals, and took college courses.

71
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:50,314
It was the same across America.

72
00:02:50,334 --> 00:02:52,549
In other camps, German prisoners burned

73
00:02:52,569 --> 00:02:54,783
their leftovers, afraid that if the

74
00:02:54,803 --> 00:02:57,762
Americans saw them, the rations would be cut.

75
00:02:57,782 --> 00:03:01,114
One German later said he went in at one

76
00:03:01,134 --> 00:03:03,348
hundred twenty-eight pounds and came out,

77
00:03:03,368 --> 00:03:05,975
two years later, at one hundred eighty-five.

78
00:03:07,075 --> 00:03:09,372
Then the war in Europe ended.

79
00:03:09,392 --> 00:03:11,690
American troops opened the German camps,

80
00:03:11,710 --> 00:03:15,166
and the country saw what had been done to

81
00:03:15,186 --> 00:03:16,325
Allied prisoners there:

82
00:03:16,345 --> 00:03:18,256
men starved down to bone.

83
00:03:18,276 --> 00:03:20,574
Newspapers were accusing the government of

84
00:03:20,594 --> 00:03:21,732
coddling the enemy.

85
00:03:21,752 --> 00:03:24,050
Now many Americans wanted the prisoners

86
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:25,615
here to feel it.

87
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:30,681
On May fourth, 1945, the Army command in

88
00:03:30,701 --> 00:03:33,168
Dallas ordered a limit on prisoner food:

89
00:03:33,188 --> 00:03:35,654
about two thousand five hundred calories a

90
00:03:35,674 --> 00:03:38,141
day for men who did not work,

91
00:03:38,161 --> 00:03:40,628
and about three thousand five hundred for

92
00:03:40,648 --> 00:03:41,693
men who did.

93
00:03:41,713 --> 00:03:43,470
Across the country, canteens stopped

94
00:03:43,490 --> 00:03:44,180
selling food.

95
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:47,377
The War Department said there was a food shortage.

96
00:03:47,397 --> 00:03:49,509
One later study argues that public

97
00:03:49,529 --> 00:03:51,660
pressure mattered at least as much.

98
00:03:52,925 --> 00:03:55,647
Two thousand five hundred calories was a

99
00:03:55,667 --> 00:03:56,430
real cut.

100
00:03:56,450 --> 00:03:57,997
It was not starvation.

101
00:03:58,017 --> 00:04:00,738
But each camp commander decided how to

102
00:04:00,758 --> 00:04:02,325
carry out the order.

103
00:04:04,025 --> 00:04:06,472
Hereford's commander was Colonel Joseph Carvolth.

104
00:04:06,492 --> 00:04:09,761
Before Hereford, he had run the big camp

105
00:04:09,781 --> 00:04:11,816
for German prisoners at Mexia.

106
00:04:11,836 --> 00:04:15,125
At Hereford, he went far beyond his orders.

107
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:19,598
He closed the canteen to snacks and drinks.

108
00:04:19,618 --> 00:04:22,766
He sent back the food parcels that arrived

109
00:04:22,786 --> 00:04:23,954
for the prisoners.

110
00:04:23,974 --> 00:04:26,726
Letters of complaint were read inside the

111
00:04:26,746 --> 00:04:28,310
camp and thrown away.

112
00:04:28,330 --> 00:04:30,990
The camp physician, Major Luigi Cabitto,

113
00:04:31,010 --> 00:04:33,813
reported that the limit of two thousand

114
00:04:33,833 --> 00:04:36,233
five hundred calories was rarely reached.

115
00:04:36,253 --> 00:04:39,460
Many days the menus came to about two

116
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:41,900
thousand, and sometimes only fifteen hundred.

117
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:44,927
The officers' compound was hit hardest.

118
00:04:44,947 --> 00:04:48,057
A history of the camp later described it

119
00:04:48,077 --> 00:04:50,404
as living under a retaliatory starvation

120
00:04:50,424 --> 00:04:54,125
order, from April to December 1945.

121
00:04:55,225 --> 00:04:57,130
The prisoners had a name for it:

122
00:04:57,150 --> 00:04:58,865
la fame.

123
00:04:58,885 --> 00:04:59,965
The hunger.

124
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:04,047
They hunted anything that moved inside the wire.

125
00:05:04,067 --> 00:05:05,731
Dogs and cats disappeared.

126
00:05:05,751 --> 00:05:08,256
Men caught rattlesnakes, birds and lizards.

127
00:05:08,276 --> 00:05:12,045
Some bought hair oil at the camp store and

128
00:05:12,065 --> 00:05:14,991
used it to fry grasshoppers on their

129
00:05:15,011 --> 00:05:15,833
barracks stoves.

130
00:05:15,853 --> 00:05:18,358
Others boiled fish heads, grass and

131
00:05:18,378 --> 00:05:19,621
wildflowers into broth.

132
00:05:19,641 --> 00:05:22,988
Men who worked in the potato fields boiled

133
00:05:23,008 --> 00:05:24,251
potatoes out there.

134
00:05:24,271 --> 00:05:27,618
Enlisted men threw food over the fence to

135
00:05:27,638 --> 00:05:28,480
the officers.

136
00:05:29,875 --> 00:05:32,521
By the fall, the men were hollow-eyed,

137
00:05:32,541 --> 00:05:33,663
thin and listless.

138
00:05:33,683 --> 00:05:36,710
Many had given up soccer, writing and painting.

139
00:05:36,730 --> 00:05:39,015
They no longer had the energy.

140
00:05:40,725 --> 00:05:43,375
And yet, in August 1945, the prisoners

141
00:05:43,395 --> 00:05:46,209
held an art exhibition in an empty

142
00:05:46,229 --> 00:05:46,614
barracks:

143
00:05:46,634 --> 00:05:48,234
paintings of the Panhandle,

144
00:05:48,254 --> 00:05:51,068
portraits, carvings, rings hammered out of coins.

145
00:05:51,088 --> 00:05:53,902
One of the visitors was Father John

146
00:05:53,922 --> 00:05:55,927
Krukkert, a Dutch-born priest of

147
00:05:55,947 --> 00:05:57,546
fifty-five who served St.

148
00:05:57,566 --> 00:05:59,571
Mary's Catholic Church in Umbarger,

149
00:05:59,591 --> 00:06:02,425
a small farm town halfway to Amarillo.

150
00:06:03,725 --> 00:06:04,092
St.

151
00:06:04,112 --> 00:06:06,026
Mary's had bare white walls.

152
00:06:06,046 --> 00:06:09,121
Father Krukkert asked the Army to let a

153
00:06:09,141 --> 00:06:11,442
few of the prisoners decorate them.

154
00:06:11,462 --> 00:06:13,376
He would supply the materials.

155
00:06:13,396 --> 00:06:16,471
The women of the parish would cook the

156
00:06:16,491 --> 00:06:18,425
painters a meal every day.

157
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:22,393
One of the men chosen was Lieutenant

158
00:06:22,413 --> 00:06:23,556
Franco Di Bello.

159
00:06:23,576 --> 00:06:25,882
He was twenty-three, a career officer

160
00:06:25,902 --> 00:06:29,280
captured in North Africa in 1943,

161
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:31,415
an athlete who spoke good English.

162
00:06:31,435 --> 00:06:32,839
Like everyone at Hereford,

163
00:06:32,859 --> 00:06:34,618
he had refused to collaborate.

164
00:06:34,638 --> 00:06:37,465
Accounts differ on the size of the team:

165
00:06:37,485 --> 00:06:40,668
seven men in one book, nine in a later

166
00:06:40,688 --> 00:06:41,488
magazine story.

167
00:06:43,125 --> 00:06:45,261
Meanwhile, the camp chaplain had alerted

168
00:06:45,281 --> 00:06:47,416
the Bishop of Amarillo, Laurence FitzSimon.

169
00:06:47,436 --> 00:06:49,572
That was why, on September seventh,

170
00:06:49,592 --> 00:06:52,446
the bishop sat down to lunch in the

171
00:06:52,466 --> 00:06:53,266
prisoners' mess.

172
00:06:54,475 --> 00:06:56,243
He wrote to his congressman,

173
00:06:56,263 --> 00:06:58,390
Eugene Worley, and described the meal:

174
00:06:58,410 --> 00:07:00,536
the watery soup, the salty herring,

175
00:07:00,556 --> 00:07:01,967
the bread and water.

176
00:07:01,987 --> 00:07:03,775
He asked for an investigation.

177
00:07:05,625 --> 00:07:07,990
In late October, the work at St.

178
00:07:08,010 --> 00:07:08,671
Mary's began.

179
00:07:08,691 --> 00:07:11,056
Every morning an Army truck carried the

180
00:07:11,076 --> 00:07:14,122
painters out of the camp, past the town of

181
00:07:14,142 --> 00:07:16,166
Hereford, to Umbarger, twenty-six miles away.

182
00:07:16,186 --> 00:07:18,891
The man at the wheel was Sergeant John

183
00:07:18,911 --> 00:07:20,935
Coyle, a skinny young man from

184
00:07:20,955 --> 00:07:23,320
Pennsylvania who had left school in the

185
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:26,065
eighth grade to work in a sock mill.

186
00:07:27,375 --> 00:07:29,799
Upstairs, the prisoners climbed the scaffolds.

187
00:07:29,819 --> 00:07:31,835
Downstairs, in the church basement,

188
00:07:31,855 --> 00:07:34,686
the women of Umbarger laid out dinner:

189
00:07:34,706 --> 00:07:36,722
roasts, hams, sausages, homemade bread,

190
00:07:36,742 --> 00:07:37,944
sauerkraut, mashed potatoes,

191
00:07:37,964 --> 00:07:41,610
and pies made with cherries from their own trees.

192
00:07:41,630 --> 00:07:44,460
The parish was made up of German-American

193
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:45,295
farm families.

194
00:07:46,675 --> 00:07:49,406
The Italians ate so much that Father

195
00:07:49,426 --> 00:07:50,585
Krukkert grew worried.

196
00:07:50,605 --> 00:07:53,337
He was afraid they would hurt themselves.

197
00:07:53,357 --> 00:07:55,715
Over the weeks, they slowed down.

198
00:07:57,375 --> 00:07:59,696
Sergeant Coyle knew the men back in the

199
00:07:59,716 --> 00:08:00,867
camp were still hungry.

200
00:08:00,887 --> 00:08:03,500
So he borrowed a shotgun, sat on the front

201
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:05,256
fender of a farmer's Model A,

202
00:08:05,276 --> 00:08:07,012
and shot jackrabbits in the pastures,

203
00:08:07,032 --> 00:08:08,495
where they were a pest.

204
00:08:09,850 --> 00:08:12,505
He and the farmer skinned and cleaned them.

205
00:08:12,525 --> 00:08:14,845
The painters hung the rabbits inside their

206
00:08:14,865 --> 00:08:17,520
trouser legs, on strings tied to their belts.

207
00:08:17,540 --> 00:08:20,195
In the evening Coyle drove them back to

208
00:08:20,215 --> 00:08:22,869
the camp, and they walked the rabbits past

209
00:08:22,889 --> 00:08:24,541
the guards at the gate.

210
00:08:24,561 --> 00:08:27,216
That night, a few hungry men ate roasted

211
00:08:27,236 --> 00:08:28,036
jackrabbit.

212
00:08:28,850 --> 00:08:31,375
If he had been caught, Coyle could have

213
00:08:31,395 --> 00:08:33,920
lost his stripes and gone to the guardhouse.

214
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:35,530
The smuggling went on anyway.

215
00:08:37,250 --> 00:08:38,935
The bishop's letter worked.

216
00:08:38,955 --> 00:08:41,066
Investigators came from the Italian

217
00:08:41,086 --> 00:08:44,476
embassy, the Red Cross and the State Department.

218
00:08:44,496 --> 00:08:47,460
They found that over about seven months,

219
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:50,444
the prisoners at Hereford had lost an

220
00:08:50,464 --> 00:08:52,575
average of twenty pounds each.

221
00:08:52,595 --> 00:08:54,300
Some had lost forty.

222
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,344
The Army also looked into itself.

223
00:08:57,364 --> 00:09:00,102
In a recorded phone call, an assistant

224
00:09:00,122 --> 00:09:02,466
provost marshal general in Washington told

225
00:09:02,486 --> 00:09:05,618
Carvolth not to worry, that he was going

226
00:09:05,638 --> 00:09:07,194
to bat for him.

227
00:09:07,214 --> 00:09:09,558
Carvolth told investigators he had not

228
00:09:09,578 --> 00:09:11,528
known his prisoners were hungry.

229
00:09:11,548 --> 00:09:13,892
But the complaints were largely confirmed.

230
00:09:13,912 --> 00:09:15,862
By November the canteen reopened,

231
00:09:15,882 --> 00:09:18,640
and the rations went up, a little.

232
00:09:20,375 --> 00:09:21,999
The painting at St.

233
00:09:22,019 --> 00:09:24,054
Mary's took forty-one working days.

234
00:09:24,074 --> 00:09:26,931
On the walls, the prisoners painted the

235
00:09:26,951 --> 00:09:28,575
Annunciation and the Visitation,

236
00:09:28,595 --> 00:09:31,041
scenes from the life of Mary,

237
00:09:31,061 --> 00:09:33,507
inside Renaissance halls with marble floors.

238
00:09:33,527 --> 00:09:35,973
Through the arches, instead of Italian

239
00:09:35,993 --> 00:09:38,850
hills, you see High Plains wheat fields

240
00:09:38,870 --> 00:09:40,083
and Texas sky.

241
00:09:40,103 --> 00:09:42,960
The farmhouses in the distance belong to

242
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:45,035
the families who fed them.

243
00:09:46,250 --> 00:09:49,041
Two wood carvers cut the Last Supper into

244
00:09:49,061 --> 00:09:50,799
the front of the altar.

245
00:09:50,819 --> 00:09:53,610
A large oil painting of the Assumption of

246
00:09:53,630 --> 00:09:55,367
Mary was hung as well.

247
00:09:55,387 --> 00:09:58,517
In December 1945, the work was dedicated.

248
00:09:58,537 --> 00:10:01,119
The artists sat in the front row,

249
00:10:01,139 --> 00:10:03,370
and Sergeant Coyle sat with them.

250
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,283
That same autumn, prisoners built a small

251
00:10:07,303 --> 00:10:09,914
chapel inside the camp for the five men

252
00:10:09,934 --> 00:10:11,559
who had died at Hereford.

253
00:10:11,579 --> 00:10:13,862
On the floor, in Italian, they carved:

254
00:10:13,882 --> 00:10:15,835
from the Italian prisoners to the

255
00:10:15,855 --> 00:10:17,500
companions who will not return.

256
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:22,307
In January 1946, the last prisoners left

257
00:10:22,327 --> 00:10:24,665
Hereford on troop trains, on their way

258
00:10:24,685 --> 00:10:25,675
home to Italy.

259
00:10:25,695 --> 00:10:28,033
The camp was declared surplus and its

260
00:10:28,053 --> 00:10:29,400
barracks were sold off.

261
00:10:30,650 --> 00:10:33,470
Franco Di Bello went back into the army,

262
00:10:33,490 --> 00:10:35,955
and rose to the rank of general.

263
00:10:35,975 --> 00:10:39,560
In 1981, a Texas newspaperman named Donald

264
00:10:39,580 --> 00:10:42,234
Williams wrote to him about the paintings.

265
00:10:42,254 --> 00:10:44,908
That was how Williams first learned about

266
00:10:44,928 --> 00:10:46,710
la fame.

267
00:10:46,730 --> 00:10:49,925
His book about it came out in 1992.

268
00:10:51,025 --> 00:10:53,252
If someone in your family was held at

269
00:10:53,272 --> 00:10:55,218
Hereford, guarded it, or sat at those

270
00:10:55,238 --> 00:10:57,485
tables in Umbarger, tell us in the comments.

271
00:10:58,850 --> 00:11:00,607
The camp is gone now.

272
00:11:00,627 --> 00:11:03,450
A water tower still stands in the fields

